From: Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 11:05:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: q6asm-dai: playback periods up to 122880 bytes

Each playback period is one ASM write and one WRITE_DONE event, which
wakes the CPU and the sound server. With 65536 bytes, and the driver's
480-frame step, the longest period of 48 kHz stereo S16 was 16320 frames
(340 ms), about three wake-ups a second. The downstream driver for the
same ADSP firmware family (msm-pcm-q6-v2.h, the Pro1X's vendor kernel
msm-4.19 "bengal") allows 122880 bytes: 30720 frames, 640 ms, a multiple
of 480, so a deep-buffer stream needs about 1.5 wake-ups a second. The
compress path already writes fragments of up to 128 KiB.

The buffer limit follows (8 periods, 960 KiB, 5.1 s), and with it the
buffer each MultiMedia PCM substream gets preallocated (pcm_new): 960 KiB
instead of 512 KiB per substream. Short periods stay as they were.

diff --git a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6asm-dai.c b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6asm-dai.c
index 4f09fdd409..28a75e9d18 100644
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6asm-dai.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6asm-dai.c
@@ -26,7 +26,13 @@
 
 #define PLAYBACK_MIN_NUM_PERIODS    2
 #define PLAYBACK_MAX_NUM_PERIODS   8
-#define PLAYBACK_MAX_PERIOD_SIZE    65536
+/*
+ * Each period is one ASM write to the DSP and one WRITE_DONE back, i.e. a
+ * wake-up of the CPU. 122880 bytes as the downstream msm-pcm-q6-v2 driver
+ * allows (640 ms of 48 kHz stereo S16, a multiple of the 480-frame step),
+ * so that a deep-buffer stream wakes the CPU about 1.5 times a second.
+ */
+#define PLAYBACK_MAX_PERIOD_SIZE    122880
 #define PLAYBACK_MIN_PERIOD_SIZE    128
 #define CAPTURE_MIN_NUM_PERIODS     2
 #define CAPTURE_MAX_NUM_PERIODS     8
